            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A divinely appointed era of redemption, grace, and liberation, frequently rooted in the Year of Jubilee and fulfilling the 'acceptable year of the Lord'.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Isaiah 61:2\",\"Luke 4:19\",\"Leviticus 25:10\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A symbolic unit of prophetic time where one day represents one literal year (the day-year principle).\",\"basis\":[\"Numbers 14:34\",\"Ezekiel 4:6\",\"Daniel 9:24-27\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A representation of God's eternal nature and perspective on time, where human years are vastly different from divine time (e.g., a thousand years as one day).\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 90:4\",\"2 Peter 3:8\",\"Psalm 102:27\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A specific, often symbolic, long duration of divine administration, judgment, or reign, such as the Millennium.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 20:2-7\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The symbolic meaning of 'year' often depends heavily on context. Soteriologically, it represents the era of Christ's grace (Jubilee). Eschatologically, it is frequently debated whether 'years' denote literal timeframes, symbolic epochs of completion, or apply a prophetic day-year calculus.\"}",
